1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,330
Right. Yep. And we're looking at a really, uh,

2
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:08,250
counterintuitive concept today. Basically, why documenting exactly what a method cannot do

3
00:00:08,990 --> 00:00:10,310
is actually its greatest asset.

4
00:00:10,830 --> 00:00:13,150
It sounds a bit backwards at first, I know.

5
00:00:13,290 --> 00:00:16,210
It really does. I mean, I have to push back on this right away.

6
00:00:16,910 --> 00:00:21,310
When you are adopting a new framework for your ministry or, you know, your program,

7
00:00:22,150 --> 00:00:24,049
you usually only get the shiny sales pitch.

8
00:00:24,270 --> 00:00:26,790
Oh, absolutely. Everything is pitched as a silver bullet.

9
00:00:26,870 --> 00:00:35,170
Exactly. So why on earth would a method explicitly advertise its own limitations in writing and just lay out all its potential failures?

10
00:00:35,650 --> 00:00:37,590
Well, think about it from a leadership perspective.

11
00:00:38,070 --> 00:00:43,070
Before relying on any method, a competent manager always asks what could go wrong.

12
00:00:43,190 --> 00:00:44,610
Sure. You have to know the risks.

13
00:00:44,730 --> 00:00:47,870
Right. And, uh, the SDD method answers this directly.

14
00:00:48,190 --> 00:00:52,970
In fact, reading these explicitly written limits is literally mandatory before you even start using it.

15
00:00:53,150 --> 00:00:56,250
Wow. Mandatory. Okay, well, let's get into the specifics then.

16
00:00:56,909 --> 00:01:02,470
Let's number aloud the four major limits this method claims because these are what matter most to a manager.

17
00:01:02,610 --> 00:01:03,950
What is limit number one?

18
00:01:04,610 --> 00:01:09,330
So limit number one: it does not claim that its standards have been shown to work well together yet.

19
00:01:09,850 --> 00:01:10,830
Wait, really?

20
00:01:11,450 --> 00:01:15,910
Because, like, if I buy a car, I fully expect the engine and the transmission to work together.

21
00:01:16,630 --> 00:01:20,190
How can a manager trust a system where the standards aren't proven to integrate?

22
00:01:20,770 --> 00:01:25,510
It is a massive risk because no one has tested all the standards together at once.

23
00:01:25,910 --> 00:01:29,850
Parts of your program could easily become, um, fragmented or siloed.

24
00:01:30,370 --> 00:01:32,670
Or even duplicated across different departments?

25
00:01:33,010 --> 00:01:35,650
Exactly, which brings us directly to limit number two.

26
00:01:36,150 --> 00:01:39,930
It does not claim that every standard has been tested by use on a real project.

27
00:01:40,470 --> 00:01:42,150
So it's basically unproven in the field.

28
00:01:42,850 --> 00:01:47,730
In many ways, yeah. And then limit number three: a program does not check every step.

29
00:01:48,570 --> 00:01:50,630
Meaning there is still a huge human element.

30
00:01:51,170 --> 00:01:54,530
Yeah. Important handovers still rely entirely on a person.

31
00:01:54,890 --> 00:01:57,130
Okay. Got it. And what is the fourth limit?

32
00:01:57,590 --> 00:02:03,870
Number four: it is not cheap. The method relies on 12 different documents, which means there are, uh,

33
00:02:04,650 --> 00:02:06,810
many places for corrections to be made.

34
00:02:07,290 --> 00:02:09,550
And I'm guessing that gets costly to change?

35
00:02:10,290 --> 00:02:13,730
Very costly to change, especially if discipline is not strictly maintained.

36
00:02:14,030 --> 00:02:14,230
Right.

37
00:02:14,910 --> 00:02:19,350
Now, beyond those four operational limits, let's talk about what is entirely missing.

38
00:02:19,870 --> 00:02:22,770
There are two big ones, so let's number those aloud too.

39
00:02:23,110 --> 00:02:24,830
What is the first missing standard?

40
00:02:25,370 --> 00:02:26,750
Number one is accessibility,

41
00:02:27,510 --> 00:02:30,470
and number two is services in more than one language.

42
00:02:31,130 --> 00:02:35,170
Those seem like massive oversights for any government service you might be running.

43
00:02:35,830 --> 00:02:43,230
They do. But here is the crucial part. If those requirements aren't specifically written into the, uh, register of what was asked,

44
00:02:43,870 --> 00:02:44,990
they simply will not be built.

45
00:02:45,650 --> 00:02:46,870
They just get ignored entirely.

46
00:02:47,270 --> 00:02:52,150
Yes. If you don't explicitly ask for them in that register, your program will lack them completely.

47
00:02:52,950 --> 00:02:57,190
So how does a manager actually handle this reality when briefing leadership?

48
00:02:57,350 --> 00:02:59,670
Like, we have the Progressa example, right?

49
00:03:00,490 --> 00:03:05,370
Yes. Looking at Progressa, and just a reminder, that is the fictional country of these examples.

50
00:03:05,630 --> 00:03:06,950
Right. A totally fictional country.

51
00:03:07,290 --> 00:03:13,170
But in their scenario, the head of ICT writes a briefing to the minister about adopting the SDD method.

52
00:03:13,570 --> 00:03:15,070
And what exactly do they say?

53
00:03:15,550 --> 00:03:18,230
They quote all seven limits exactly as written.

54
00:03:18,390 --> 00:03:19,510
They don't hide anything.

55
00:03:20,010 --> 00:03:23,770
Wow. So they make sure the minister decides with full knowledge of the risks.

56
00:03:24,110 --> 00:03:27,110
Exactly. It ensures absolute transparency.

57
00:03:27,690 --> 00:03:33,310
A list of limits looks like a weakness, but a method that hides its limits teaches the wrong lesson.

58
00:03:33,750 --> 00:03:36,510
Because if you know the limit, you can actually plan around it.

59
00:03:36,930 --> 00:03:39,030
Yes. A stated limit can be planned around.

60
00:03:39,150 --> 00:03:42,590
An unstated limit is only found after the money is already spent.

61
00:03:43,030 --> 00:03:45,070
And by then, it is way too late.

62
00:03:45,870 --> 00:03:47,850
That is such a powerful takeaway for today.

